WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.060
Do you ever feel stressed about money?

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:14.460
Unless you’re a prince or a preschooler,
you probably said yes.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:18.900
For a lot of people, it always feels like
there’s not quite enough to go around.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:23.080
You’re constantly having to make tough decisions
about what you can forego, and it never feels

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:25.490
like you’re really in control of your finances.

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:30.780
Well, when you need a bit of inspiration,
look no further than the front of a one dollar bill.

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.500
I’m talking about this guy: George Washington.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.600
Besides being the first president of the United
States, General George Washington was the

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:41.220
commander of the rag-tag Continental Army during the
Revolutionary War.

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:46.260
He must’ve felt like he never had enough
soldiers to go around, but by making a plan,

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:51.470
he was able to lead that army to victory over
the greatest fighting force in the world.

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:56.010
You can take a cue from George by becoming commander-in-chief of your finances.

00:00:56.010 --> 00:01:00.720
Each one of these is a soldier in your army,
and your battle plan is a budget.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:04.330
I know, the word “budget” makes most people
want to go AWOL.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:10.400
In fact, according to a 2016 Gallup poll,
only 32% of couples keep a written budget of any kind.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:14.600
But if you were a soldier, would you want
to follow a general who was making it up as

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:15.649
he went along?

00:01:15.649 --> 00:01:19.930
A lot of people tend to think of budgeting
as prediction--estimating what you’ll make

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:22.100
in future months and how you’ll want to
spend it.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:26.259
But the most effective budgets work exclusively
with present dollars.

00:01:26.259 --> 00:01:29.869
After all, you can’t give orders to soldiers
that don’t exist!

00:01:29.869 --> 00:01:35.450
So the size of your army is only how much
money you currently have in your bank accounts.

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:40.039
And as general, your role is to give every
last one of those soldiers a job to do.

00:01:40.039 --> 00:01:45.429
So, if you have a total of $10,000 across
all of your accounts, you need to decide how

00:01:45.429 --> 00:01:50.340
much of that you’re going to spend this
month, and how much you’re going to set aside for savings.

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:54.940
Before we can decide how much we want to spend,
we need to know how much we’re spending now.

00:01:54.940 --> 00:02:00.840
Print out your last 3 months of bank statements,
and put each expense into one of 5 categories:

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:02.060
Essentials.

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:03.950
These are the things you can’t do without.

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:07.979
Rent, utilities, groceries, gas, car payments,
health insurance.

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:11.180
Anything you need to live or do your job effectively.

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:12.540
Security.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:16.740
These are expenses that increase your financial
stability, like building emergency funds or

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:17.910
paying off debts.

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:20.860
Don’t double count credit cards in this
category though!

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:24.940
If you use a card to buy groceries, that expense
should go in “Essentials”.

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:26.140
Goals.

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:30.470
This is money you set aside to achieve big
life goals, like saving up to buy a house,

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:33.860
starting your own business, or taking a special
vacation.

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:35.040
Lifestyle.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.330
These are expenses that help you navigate
your social world.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:42.390
Personal grooming and apparel, gifts for friends
and family, gym memberships, even pet needs

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:43.680
can go in here.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:44.680
Discretionary.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:48.000
This category is all about treating yourself.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.460
Going out to dinner, renting a movie, buying
a video game, or getting a massage.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:56.861
Everyone has their own priorities, but think
of this as a good battle plan template, with

00:02:56.861 --> 00:03:01.270
the most urgent needs at the top, and the
more flexible ones towards the bottom.

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:05.340
Now that you know how much you generally spend
on these categories, you can start assigning

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:07.300
your soldiers to next month’s duties.

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:11.570
Unless you’re living paycheck to paycheck,
you probably have some money left over--but

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:14.320
don’t let those soldiers just sit around!

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:16.240
Decide what you’re saving them for.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:19.360
Is it an emergency fund or a Hawaiian vacation?

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:23.190
Savings that have explicit purposes are a
lot harder to steal from.

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:24.190
Congratulations!

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:27.300
You’ve made your first month’s budget
Now how do you make it stick?

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:28.640
Number one.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:29.780
Write it down.

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:34.810
You can use a spreadsheet, an app, or good
old pencil and paper, but no general ever

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:38.260
won a war by keeping all their strategy up here.

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:39.240
Number two.

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:40.280
Update it.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:45.620
Conditions on the ground will change, so restrategize
at the beginning of every month--and you’ll

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:48.480
have new soldiers to deploy in next month’s
battles.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:49.840
Number three.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:51.700
Use advanced technology.

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:55.550
Thanks to the dozens of budgeting apps you
can download for your phone, no one has to

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:57.790
keep a checkbook longhand anymore.

00:03:57.790 --> 00:04:02.580
This can be especially helpful for discretionary
spending: now you can know right away whether

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:05.160
you can afford that Weird Al album on vinyl.

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:06.830
So how you do know you’re budgeting correctly?

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:10.970
Well, the most obvious answer is that you’re
less stressed.

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:15.819
Imagine enjoying a dinner out without worrying
about whether or not you can afford it.

00:04:15.819 --> 00:04:19.570
Imagine not holding your breath every time
you check your account balance.

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:23.910
You’d be amazed how much you can improve
your mood just by introducing more clarity

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:25.880
and control into your finances.

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:30.190
Like a lot of good financial habits, the point
of budgeting isn’t to have more money…

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:33.120
it’s to be happier with the money you have.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:34.700
And that’s our two cents!

